<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shimazu Nariakira</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1809">1809</a>–<a href="/years/1858">1858</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shimazu Nariakira was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satsuma-domain">Satsuma Domain</a> from 1851 to 1858 and one of the most forward-looking lords of late Tokugawa <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. He built Western-style reverberatory furnaces, shipyards, glassworks and factories at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shuseikan">Shuseikan</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagoshima">Kagoshima</a>, and promoted able retainers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saigo-takamori">Saigo Takamori</a>. His modernisation prepared Satsuma to become one of the leaders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shimazu-nariakira.jpg" alt="Shimazu Nariakira" width="320" height="435" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hannah · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shimazu_Nariakira.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Nariakira was born on 28 April 1809 at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satsuma-domain">Satsuma</a> estate in Edo, the son of Shimazu Narioki, and was named heir at the age of three. Like his grandfather Shigehide, he was drawn to Western learning: he studied Shigehide's clocks, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telescope">telescopes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/microscope">microscopes</a>, learned Roman letters, which he later used as a private code, and met the German physician Philipp Franz von Siebold. His father and the chief adviser Zusho Hirosato distrusted his costly plans, and his father's consort <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugra">Yura</a> pressed the claims of her son, his half-brother Shimazu Hisamitsu.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1846 the shogunal official Abe Masahiro sent him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satsuma-domain">Satsuma</a> to deal with French and British demands for treaties with the Ryūkyū Kingdom, and the two agreed to allow such relations there rather than risk conflict. His father then sidelined him, and his followers, who blamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugra">Yura</a> for the deaths of his eldest children, were made to commit seppuku. With help from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/date-munenari">Date Munenari</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uwajima">Uwajima</a>, Abe intervened: Zusho committed seppuku on 18 December 1848 after taking the blame for illegal trade, and Narioki was pressed to retire.</p><p>As daimyō, Nariakira had the Iroha-maru, one of the first Western-style ships built in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, made by the physician Minayoshi Hotoku, and set up a shipyard at Sakurajima. He brought Western-style cavalry into the domain's army, founded the Rangaku Koshujo for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and Western studies, and in 1848 obtained the first daguerreotype camera imported into Japan. He promoted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saigo-takamori">Saigō Takamori</a> to be his attendant in 1854 and pardoned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okubo-toshimichi">Ōkubo Toshimichi</a>, who had been exiled for supporting him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In July 1858, while supervising manoeuvres at Tempozan for sending 3,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satsuma-domain">Satsuma</a> soldiers to Edo, he was overcome by the heat and died at Tsurumaru Castle. Saigō and Ōkubo took up his views, among them centring government on the emperor and Westernizing the military, which later underlay the Meiji government. He was deified as the kami Terukuni Daimyōjin. His portrait, made on 17 September 1857, is the oldest surviving daguerreotype by a Japanese photographer and in 1999 became the first photograph designated an Important Cultural Property.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shimazu Nariakira” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Zusho Hiromichi restored its finances in the 1830s, and <b>Shimazu Nariakira</b> built Western-style furnaces, shipyards and factories at the Shuseikan.</blockquote><a href="/state/satsuma-domain">Satsuma Domain</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-tokyo">Edo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagoshima">Kagoshima</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.68,139.77&amp;year=1840" aria-label="Edo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8080 875 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Shimazu Nariakira"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8530 1145h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8530 1145h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.68,139.77&amp;year=1840">What was here in 1840? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
